WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.451 --> 00:00:02.812
S3 has got to be the most commonly used and

2
00:00:02.912 --> 00:00:04.532
most loved AWS service.

3
00:00:04.873 --> 00:00:06.893
It's simple to get started with, largely cost

4
00:00:06.953 --> 00:00:08.294
effective compared to alternatives,

5
00:00:09.034 --> 00:00:10.054
and scales massively.

6
00:00:10.674 --> 00:00:11.895
But it's not a file system.

7
00:00:12.475 --> 00:00:14.476
It follows a key value object store model,

8
00:00:15.036 --> 00:00:16.636
and this makes it a bit of a misfit in

9
00:00:16.656 --> 00:00:18.717
cases when you want to use it like a standard

10
00:00:18.757 --> 00:00:21.418
folder using regular file operations.

11
00:00:22.658 --> 00:00:24.700
Now, normal file systems are usually required

12
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:27.061
for things like databases, applications that

13
00:00:27.101 --> 00:00:30.884
write append log files, web applications or CMS

14
00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:32.865
apps that assume a mounted folder for their

15
00:00:32.885 --> 00:00:33.166
data.

16
00:00:33.986 --> 00:00:36.288
And even though S3 is well supported for things

17
00:00:36.328 --> 00:00:38.670
like big data and batch processing workloads,

18
00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:40.971
it can actually become a performance bottleneck

19
00:00:41.311 --> 00:00:42.632
if you've got lots of tiny files.

20
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:46.014
Now AWS has just released S3 files.

21
00:00:46.754 --> 00:00:49.515
This is a new managed file system backed by S3.

22
00:00:50.135 --> 00:00:51.776
S3 files tries to be the sweet spot,

23
00:00:52.316 --> 00:00:54.757
giving you a proper file system with S3

24
00:00:54.797 --> 00:00:55.197
underneath.

25
00:00:56.077 --> 00:00:57.938
The promise of it is that you'll get the

26
00:00:57.958 --> 00:01:00.799
scalability, durability and cost benefits of S3

27
00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:03.120
with performance and behavior of a file system.

28
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.441
And one of the big benefits is that it

29
00:01:05.481 --> 00:01:08.444
integrates easily into EC2, ECS, and even

30
00:01:08.484 --> 00:01:10.565
Lambda, unlike some of the previous options.

31
00:01:11.726 --> 00:01:14.128
We're going to dive very deep into S3 files,

32
00:01:14.388 --> 00:01:15.709
talk you through how to use it,

33
00:01:15.869 --> 00:01:18.331
where it fits, and how it compares against all

34
00:01:18.371 --> 00:01:19.452
your file storage options.

35
00:01:20.072 --> 00:01:21.513
You'll hear us share our experience,

36
00:01:21.913 --> 00:01:24.135
since we have also been using S3 files in

37
00:01:24.255 --> 00:01:26.477
real-world projects, and we also did some

38
00:01:26.517 --> 00:01:27.097
benchmarking.

39
00:01:27.938 --> 00:01:29.479
I'm Eoin, I'm here with Luciano.

40
00:01:29.919 --> 00:01:31.901
Welcome to AWS Bites, episode 154.

41
00:01:41.622 --> 00:01:44.091
Luciano, maybe you can start off by telling

42
00:01:44.151 --> 00:01:46.841
everyone why is S3 not a file system in brief?

43
00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:48.927
Yeah, the first reason is because it doesn't

44
00:01:48.947 --> 00:01:50.548
have the concept of directories.

45
00:01:51.408 --> 00:01:53.989
What looks like a folder is basically just a key

46
00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:54.509
prefix.

47
00:01:54.789 --> 00:01:57.569
You can use the familiar slash to make it look

48
00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.050
like there are directories, but it's just naming

49
00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:01.690
conventions.

50
00:02:02.631 --> 00:02:04.591
And in that sense, it doesn't have true

51
00:02:04.671 --> 00:02:05.251
directories.

52
00:02:05.831 --> 00:02:08.752
You cannot do things like atomic moves or

53
00:02:08.772 --> 00:02:09.212
renames.

54
00:02:10.312 --> 00:02:12.573
For instance, if you just want to rename a file

55
00:02:12.613 --> 00:02:14.193
or move it from one place to another,

56
00:02:14.253 --> 00:02:16.454
which basically means change the prefix

57
00:02:16.514 --> 00:02:16.954
entirely,

58
00:02:18.035 --> 00:02:21.197
you have to effectively, even if you use the AWS

59
00:02:21.217 --> 00:02:23.739
S3 MV command, you are effectively copying the

60
00:02:23.839 --> 00:02:25.321
object from one place to another.

61
00:02:25.901 --> 00:02:28.583
And if you ever done that with a very large

62
00:02:28.643 --> 00:02:31.205
file, a very large object on S3,

63
00:02:31.245 --> 00:02:33.007
you might have noticed that it takes some time.

64
00:02:33.387 --> 00:02:35.469
While if you do that in a real file system,

65
00:02:35.589 --> 00:02:37.510
it's pretty much instantaneous because it's just

66
00:02:37.811 --> 00:02:40.833
literally renaming the file itself and not

67
00:02:40.913 --> 00:02:42.474
moving data or copying data.

68
00:02:43.655 --> 00:02:46.936
So that's another big difference, which is

69
00:02:46.976 --> 00:02:47.396
interesting.

70
00:02:47.716 --> 00:02:50.157
The other thing is that objects are immutable.

71
00:02:50.337 --> 00:02:54.278
You cannot modify a range of bytes inside an

72
00:02:54.338 --> 00:02:56.558
object like you could do in a file system.

73
00:02:57.319 --> 00:02:59.539
And in that sense, you cannot really append

74
00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:01.900
either into an existing object.

75
00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:03.962
There is a little bit of an exception,

76
00:03:04.122 --> 00:03:06.024
which is multi-part uploads.

77
00:03:06.945 --> 00:03:09.428
But it doesn't really work in the way you might

78
00:03:09.468 --> 00:03:11.931
expect from a regular file system.

79
00:03:12.371 --> 00:03:14.294
And if you want to deep dive into the details,

80
00:03:14.374 --> 00:03:17.638
we have been talking about that in episode 124.

81
00:03:18.058 --> 00:03:20.401
So go and check that out if you're curious.

82
00:03:21.962 --> 00:03:26.223
Another thing is that listing of either the

83
00:03:26.263 --> 00:03:29.305
entire bucket or a prefix can be expensive,

84
00:03:29.405 --> 00:03:31.125
or at least more expensive than a regular file

85
00:03:31.145 --> 00:03:31.506
system.

86
00:03:31.926 --> 00:03:35.147
And this is because there is no directory

87
00:03:35.207 --> 00:03:37.568
metadata in the same way that it would exist in

88
00:03:37.608 --> 00:03:38.228
a file system.

89
00:03:38.509 --> 00:03:41.010
So when you're running a list object operation,

90
00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.591
it's effectively querying over the keys in the

91
00:03:43.631 --> 00:03:43.931
bucket.

92
00:03:44.271 --> 00:03:48.113
Access control is something that exists in S3

93
00:03:48.153 --> 00:03:51.094
buckets, but again, different from what you

94
00:03:51.134 --> 00:03:53.014
would have in a POSIX file system,

95
00:03:53.054 --> 00:03:56.036
for example, because you can generally determine

96
00:03:56.276 --> 00:03:58.817
access control using IAM policies or ACLs.

97
00:04:00.018 --> 00:04:02.540
and not by setting up, I don't know,

98
00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.403
users and groups as you would do in a POSIX

99
00:04:05.443 --> 00:04:06.044
file system.

100
00:04:06.785 --> 00:04:08.627
And performance is also a little bit different

101
00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:13.472
because it's defined by how you structure your

102
00:04:13.592 --> 00:04:15.674
partitions by using prefixes.

103
00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:20.218
So if you put lots of objects in the same

104
00:04:20.238 --> 00:04:23.401
prefix, you can damage your performance.

105
00:04:24.082 --> 00:04:27.885
So it's typical to distribute your data over

106
00:04:27.925 --> 00:04:30.267
different partitions by using a bunch of

107
00:04:30.307 --> 00:04:33.009
different sub-prefixes, which makes querying S3

108
00:04:33.029 --> 00:04:33.229
faster.

109
00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:38.373
So specific tricks you will do with S3 that are

110
00:04:38.413 --> 00:04:40.315
not necessarily mapping to things you would do

111
00:04:40.355 --> 00:04:41.616
with a regular file system.

112
00:04:42.197 --> 00:04:44.278
And of course, there are a lot more subtle

113
00:04:44.338 --> 00:04:44.919
differences.

114
00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.883
And the real issue here is that there are

115
00:04:50.424 --> 00:04:52.665
applications out there that need file system

116
00:04:52.685 --> 00:04:53.266
semantics.

117
00:04:53.686 --> 00:04:54.667
So when you try to use S3,

118
00:04:55.868 --> 00:04:58.069
and mimic a file system, sometimes you might

119
00:04:58.129 --> 00:05:00.730
bump into things that don't necessarily match

120
00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.950
the abstraction that the application expects.

121
00:05:03.251 --> 00:05:04.751
So that's something to be aware.

122
00:05:05.391 --> 00:05:09.012
And we'll see today how S3 Files tries to fill

123
00:05:09.052 --> 00:05:09.473
that gap.

124
00:05:10.253 --> 00:05:12.314
But I guess before getting into S3 Files,

125
00:05:12.354 --> 00:05:14.674
which is pretty new, this is a topic that people

126
00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:17.175
have been trying to address for a long time.

127
00:05:17.675 --> 00:05:20.636
So what are the other solutions that existed for

128
00:05:20.776 --> 00:05:22.237
longer than S3 Files at least?

129
00:05:23.143 --> 00:05:26.364
Well, you have the FUSE user space file system

130
00:05:26.924 --> 00:05:28.985
option, which supports lots of file systems,

131
00:05:29.025 --> 00:05:32.006
but there's an S3FS FUSE library, which allows

132
00:05:32.026 --> 00:05:32.706
you to do that.

133
00:05:32.746 --> 00:05:34.086
A lot of people would do that in development,

134
00:05:34.426 --> 00:05:37.227
just to be able to explore buckets in a file

135
00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:37.567
system.

136
00:05:38.007 --> 00:05:41.969
There's also the very popular Python FS spec

137
00:05:42.129 --> 00:05:43.309
S3FS library.

138
00:05:43.329 --> 00:05:45.250
It's a different S3FS, which is used in a lot

139
00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:46.290
of big data applications.

140
00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:50.334
You also have Hadoop, has S3A, which is like a

141
00:05:50.394 --> 00:05:53.937
HDFS abstraction on top of S3.

142
00:05:54.738 --> 00:05:57.160
And more recently, in the last couple of years,

143
00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.503
you have MountPoint for S3, which is Amazon's

144
00:06:00.723 --> 00:06:02.145
own file system adapter.

145
00:06:02.505 --> 00:06:04.007
We did a whole episode on this,

146
00:06:04.187 --> 00:06:05.308
actually episode 95.

147
00:06:05.848 --> 00:06:07.550
In fact, this episode covers a lot of the

148
00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:10.032
options out there for mounting S3 as a file

149
00:06:10.052 --> 00:06:10.333
system.

150
00:06:10.973 --> 00:06:12.734
at least before S3 files came along.

151
00:06:12.974 --> 00:06:14.914
Now on top of that, there are actually a whole

152
00:06:14.954 --> 00:06:17.395
lot of AWS services that provide a bridge layer

153
00:06:17.435 --> 00:06:21.456
between S3 and file systems, like FSX for

154
00:06:21.696 --> 00:06:22.036
Lustre.

155
00:06:22.776 --> 00:06:24.657
Now this is a file system for really high

156
00:06:24.697 --> 00:06:27.598
performance computing with S3 as a backing data

157
00:06:27.658 --> 00:06:27.958
repo.

158
00:06:28.598 --> 00:06:30.558
If you're in the HPC space, that's one you'll

159
00:06:30.578 --> 00:06:31.679
come across quite frequently.

160
00:06:32.559 --> 00:06:34.020
There's Amazon File Cache.

161
00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.062
This is one that never really made it

162
00:06:37.162 --> 00:06:40.004
mainstream, I feel, but it's also a high

163
00:06:40.044 --> 00:06:41.485
performance option.

164
00:06:42.065 --> 00:06:45.367
It's a general caching layer for EC2 only that

165
00:06:45.407 --> 00:06:47.889
works on top of S3 or can work on top

166
00:06:47.929 --> 00:06:48.509
of NFS.

167
00:06:48.969 --> 00:06:50.490
And it's built on top of Lustre,

168
00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:52.472
which is one of those high performance file

169
00:06:52.492 --> 00:06:52.812
systems.

170
00:06:53.552 --> 00:06:55.973
The other one I can think of is Storage Gateway,

171
00:06:56.614 --> 00:06:59.355
which is this whole suite of services mostly for

172
00:06:59.415 --> 00:07:01.736
connecting on-premises storage to AWS.

173
00:07:02.317 --> 00:07:04.578
One of those is called S3 File Gateway,

174
00:07:05.178 --> 00:07:07.980
and it can present S3 as an NFS or an

175
00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:09.040
SMB file share.

176
00:07:10.141 --> 00:07:11.522
Now, we've covered that all in a previous

177
00:07:11.562 --> 00:07:14.083
episode, episode 95, so let's get straight into

178
00:07:14.223 --> 00:07:14.723
S3 files.

179
00:07:15.324 --> 00:07:15.784
How does it work?

180
00:07:16.543 --> 00:07:18.703
Yeah, what we mentioned already is that S3 Files

181
00:07:18.803 --> 00:07:21.604
makes a normal S3 bucket accessible as a shared

182
00:07:21.704 --> 00:07:22.364
file system.

183
00:07:22.824 --> 00:07:25.145
So you can still use the S3 bucket as normal,

184
00:07:25.505 --> 00:07:28.285
but you can also mount it as a file system.

185
00:07:28.625 --> 00:07:30.926
Any change you make in the file system is

186
00:07:31.106 --> 00:07:33.426
eventually reflected in the S3 bucket.

187
00:07:33.846 --> 00:07:36.187
And you can access S3 files from a bunch of

188
00:07:36.227 --> 00:07:38.727
different services in AWS, like EC2,

189
00:07:38.787 --> 00:07:41.768
ECS, EKS, and even Lambda using NFS.

190
00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:46.730
So data isn't just stored in the bucket itself,

191
00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.091
but you are effectively seeing it in those

192
00:07:49.492 --> 00:07:51.913
compute layers as if it was a file system,

193
00:07:52.113 --> 00:07:54.274
not normally available in those compute

194
00:07:54.334 --> 00:07:54.775
instances.

195
00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:58.137
The interesting thing is that the way the

196
00:07:58.197 --> 00:08:01.618
connection with S3 is managed by S3 files,

197
00:08:01.999 --> 00:08:05.180
because files can be streamed from S3 to NFS

198
00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:09.083
mount, but in some cases, the files can also be

199
00:08:09.143 --> 00:08:09.463
cached.

200
00:08:10.263 --> 00:08:13.844
And this is by default the case for smaller

201
00:08:13.904 --> 00:08:14.284
files.

202
00:08:14.845 --> 00:08:16.825
So the idea is that the first time you are

203
00:08:16.845 --> 00:08:18.626
accessing that file through the file system

204
00:08:18.686 --> 00:08:21.127
mount, the file is going to be streamed,

205
00:08:21.367 --> 00:08:24.008
but also cached in an intermediate layer,

206
00:08:24.048 --> 00:08:26.588
which is going to give you increased throughput

207
00:08:26.648 --> 00:08:27.449
and lower latency.

208
00:08:28.589 --> 00:08:30.150
So effectively, the next time you're going to

209
00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:33.893
try to access that same file, the read is going

210
00:08:33.953 --> 00:08:35.614
to be much faster and you will have a much

211
00:08:35.634 --> 00:08:36.295
higher throughput.

212
00:08:36.715 --> 00:08:39.957
And this is something you need to be aware

213
00:08:40.017 --> 00:08:41.298
because it's one of those things you can

214
00:08:41.338 --> 00:08:42.619
configure for performance.

215
00:08:42.639 --> 00:08:44.300
So it really depends on your use cases,

216
00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:45.861
the size of the files you are managing,

217
00:08:45.981 --> 00:08:48.063
what do you want to be in the cache versus

218
00:08:48.103 --> 00:08:50.204
what do you want to always be streamed directly

219
00:08:50.224 --> 00:08:50.745
from S3.

220
00:08:51.105 --> 00:08:53.306
But by default, files smaller than 128 kilobytes

221
00:08:53.326 --> 00:08:53.526
are cached.

222
00:08:55.988 --> 00:08:57.771
And yeah, if you need to change that,

223
00:08:57.831 --> 00:08:58.432
you can do it.

224
00:08:58.673 --> 00:09:01.998
And you should benchmark your use cases to see

225
00:09:02.078 --> 00:09:04.482
if that actually improves performance or even

226
00:09:04.542 --> 00:09:06.045
changes your cost trade-off.

227
00:09:06.165 --> 00:09:08.229
We'll talk more about costs in a second.

228
00:09:09.322 --> 00:09:11.623
Now, EFS is used under the hood to provide the

229
00:09:11.663 --> 00:09:12.383
caching layer.

230
00:09:12.463 --> 00:09:15.524
So you can, like your mental model could be like

231
00:09:16.524 --> 00:09:20.085
it's going from S3 to EFS and then from EFS

232
00:09:20.425 --> 00:09:22.866
to the compute level if you are using this

233
00:09:22.986 --> 00:09:23.966
caching mechanism.

234
00:09:24.186 --> 00:09:26.047
Otherwise, it's just streamed directly from S3.

235
00:09:27.993 --> 00:09:32.694
And this is probably why you can see that this

236
00:09:32.714 --> 00:09:35.015
storage is available for Lambda as well,

237
00:09:35.095 --> 00:09:37.156
because as you know, EFS is also something you

238
00:09:37.176 --> 00:09:38.116
can use with Lambda.

239
00:09:38.476 --> 00:09:41.197
So in general, everywhere where you can use EFS,

240
00:09:41.837 --> 00:09:44.978
it's very easy to see why they made S3 files

241
00:09:45.018 --> 00:09:45.398
available.

242
00:09:46.244 --> 00:09:47.905
Now, with all of that being said,

243
00:09:48.105 --> 00:09:49.105
how do we get started?

244
00:09:49.285 --> 00:09:52.326
Yeah, the ingredients list for this is not too

245
00:09:52.366 --> 00:09:53.287
long, actually.

246
00:09:53.607 --> 00:09:56.008
So you need an S3 bucket, if that wasn't already

247
00:09:56.068 --> 00:09:56.388
obvious.

248
00:09:56.788 --> 00:09:58.889
And then your next thing you'll create is the S3

249
00:09:58.909 --> 00:09:59.909
files file system.

250
00:10:00.469 --> 00:10:02.490
This is a resource that's linked to your bucket

251
00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:04.211
and a file system IAM role.

252
00:10:06.123 --> 00:10:07.704
And you can either link it with a specific

253
00:10:07.744 --> 00:10:09.765
prefix in your bucket rather than the whole

254
00:10:09.785 --> 00:10:10.066
bucket.

255
00:10:11.687 --> 00:10:13.848
And then within this file system resource,

256
00:10:13.888 --> 00:10:16.430
you define a couple of rules.

257
00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:17.850
So you've got the expiration rules,

258
00:10:18.351 --> 00:10:20.072
which say how long data hangs around in the

259
00:10:20.092 --> 00:10:20.372
cache.

260
00:10:20.952 --> 00:10:22.714
I think it's 24 hours by default.

261
00:10:22.774 --> 00:10:24.274
But you can set it up to,

262
00:10:24.295 --> 00:10:25.515
oh, I can't remember.

263
00:10:25.695 --> 00:10:26.816
But it's a lot longer than that.

264
00:10:28.497 --> 00:10:30.680
So you can have your expiration rules,

265
00:10:30.881 --> 00:10:32.343
and then you have the import rules.

266
00:10:32.723 --> 00:10:35.006
The import rules allow you to say what the

267
00:10:35.087 --> 00:10:37.590
maximum file size is for cached data.

268
00:10:37.911 --> 00:10:40.234
So that's 128 kilobytes by default,

269
00:10:40.274 --> 00:10:42.417
but you can set it to higher than that if

270
00:10:42.457 --> 00:10:42.657
you like.

271
00:10:43.839 --> 00:10:46.862
And you say whether the data is imported

272
00:10:46.942 --> 00:10:49.364
automatically when a directory is first accessed

273
00:10:50.225 --> 00:10:51.987
or whether the file is first accessed.

274
00:10:52.107 --> 00:10:53.749
So that could be quite useful in that if you

275
00:10:53.789 --> 00:10:56.472
access one file, S3 files can go ahead and

276
00:10:56.512 --> 00:10:58.794
import everything else in the directory within

277
00:10:58.814 --> 00:11:00.596
the size threshold, if you like.

278
00:11:01.016 --> 00:11:02.978
And these rules can be, these import rules can

279
00:11:03.018 --> 00:11:04.139
be different for different folders.

280
00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:07.842
It's worth mentioning that the IAM policy or the

281
00:11:07.862 --> 00:11:10.044
file system policy we mentioned, it needs to

282
00:11:10.124 --> 00:11:13.046
trust elastic filesystem.amazonaws.com.

283
00:11:13.186 --> 00:11:14.126
So that'll give you a

284
00:11:14.166 --> 00:11:14.547
hint about

285
00:11:14.587 --> 00:11:17.449
how tightly integrated it is with EFS,

286
00:11:17.869 --> 00:11:20.591
but it also needs EventBridge and S3 access.

287
00:11:20.831 --> 00:11:23.513
So S3 files uses EventBridge under the hood for

288
00:11:23.573 --> 00:11:24.353
S3 notifications.

289
00:11:25.394 --> 00:11:28.576
The next thing you need is a mount target.

290
00:11:28.977 --> 00:11:30.958
And this is the network link between your S3

291
00:11:30.978 --> 00:11:33.020
files and the subnets in your VPC.

292
00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.822
You need one of these for each availability zone

293
00:11:35.862 --> 00:11:36.562
you want to support.

294
00:11:37.143 --> 00:11:39.284
So you'll provide for each of these mount

295
00:11:39.324 --> 00:11:41.166
targets a subnet, security groups,

296
00:11:41.586 --> 00:11:44.168
and then say whether you want to support IPv4 or

297
00:11:44.248 --> 00:11:44.268
v6.

298
00:11:46.089 --> 00:11:48.492
And last thing, you can also create an access

299
00:11:48.532 --> 00:11:48.752
point.

300
00:11:49.393 --> 00:11:51.275
If you don't do this, you just mount the file

301
00:11:51.295 --> 00:11:53.658
system itself, but access points allow you to

302
00:11:53.698 --> 00:11:56.141
provide specific directories as the root and

303
00:11:56.221 --> 00:12:00.366
provide specific POSIX user UID and group ID.

304
00:12:00.986 --> 00:12:03.709
So mount targets and access points look exactly

305
00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:05.592
like the same things you have in EFS,

306
00:12:06.152 --> 00:12:06.974
They have the same name.

307
00:12:07.455 --> 00:12:09.238
The file system resource is quite similar too.

308
00:12:09.798 --> 00:12:12.503
But instead of configuring the EFS performance

309
00:12:12.543 --> 00:12:14.025
and throughput options, which you don't have

310
00:12:14.065 --> 00:12:16.870
here, you just link to your bucket and set the

311
00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:18.473
import and expiration rules.

312
00:12:20.212 --> 00:12:21.954
And as I mentioned, since this is EFS,

313
00:12:22.555 --> 00:12:24.157
it's a service that you have to access over the

314
00:12:24.197 --> 00:12:24.658
VPC.

315
00:12:25.038 --> 00:12:27.381
There's no public endpoint like you have with

316
00:12:27.421 --> 00:12:28.503
normal S3 access.

317
00:12:29.284 --> 00:12:31.206
For Lambda users, that means your function

318
00:12:31.286 --> 00:12:33.009
should be set up with security groups and

319
00:12:33.049 --> 00:12:33.429
subnets.

320
00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:36.433
And when it comes to mounting your file system,

321
00:12:36.814 --> 00:12:38.296
it's just like EFS.

322
00:12:39.036 --> 00:12:41.117
In Lambda, you provide the file system or the

323
00:12:41.217 --> 00:12:43.657
access point ARN and the mount path.

324
00:12:44.257 --> 00:12:46.038
And for EFS, you'll create a volume,

325
00:12:46.418 --> 00:12:48.418
a container volume from the file system or the

326
00:12:48.458 --> 00:12:48.959
access point.

327
00:12:49.439 --> 00:12:51.559
And those volumes are then mount points in your

328
00:12:51.619 --> 00:12:53.400
ECS task definitions.

329
00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:57.881
Because it uses S3, we talked about how it'll

330
00:12:57.921 --> 00:13:00.061
stream directly from S3 for large files.

331
00:13:01.041 --> 00:13:03.802
You need to have either internet access from

332
00:13:03.862 --> 00:13:06.003
your subnet or VPC endpoints to S3.

333
00:13:07.503 --> 00:13:09.886
And your security groups, because it uses NFS,

334
00:13:09.986 --> 00:13:11.867
will need the NFS port outbound.

335
00:13:12.388 --> 00:13:12.628
That's 2049.

336
00:13:13.349 --> 00:13:16.592
I think all in all, if you're used to AWS,

337
00:13:16.772 --> 00:13:18.234
it's a relatively simple one.

338
00:13:18.534 --> 00:13:21.477
It's not the most complex AWS service setup

339
00:13:21.517 --> 00:13:21.897
we've seen.

340
00:13:22.718 --> 00:13:23.679
And it's familiar, right?

341
00:13:23.739 --> 00:13:25.100
It follows EFS very closely.

342
00:13:25.781 --> 00:13:27.303
There are some more things you can configure,

343
00:13:27.423 --> 00:13:29.425
like file system policies, resource policies,

344
00:13:29.585 --> 00:13:31.006
to get tighter access control.

345
00:13:32.007 --> 00:13:34.169
So I think that's probably all we can say about

346
00:13:34.329 --> 00:13:37.031
what S3 Files is, how to set it up.

347
00:13:37.892 --> 00:13:39.073
So let's talk about how it behaves,

348
00:13:39.273 --> 00:13:40.955
maybe starting first with performance,

349
00:13:40.975 --> 00:13:42.696
because this is pretty important.

350
00:13:42.976 --> 00:13:44.958
Yeah, I'm going to read straight from the AWS

351
00:13:44.978 --> 00:13:47.060
documentation just to be sure I give you the

352
00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:47.660
right numbers.

353
00:13:48.281 --> 00:13:50.623
And basically, each file system supports up to

354
00:13:50.663 --> 00:13:53.165
five gigabytes per second of write throughput

355
00:13:53.285 --> 00:13:53.865
performance.

356
00:13:54.646 --> 00:13:56.529
And they say multiple terabytes per second of

357
00:13:56.589 --> 00:14:02.576
aggregated read throughput, up to 250k of read

358
00:14:02.737 --> 00:14:05.901
IOPS performance and 50k of write IOPS

359
00:14:05.981 --> 00:14:06.582
performance.

360
00:14:06.862 --> 00:14:09.025
The maximum per client read throughput is 3

361
00:14:09.025 --> 00:14:10.026
gigabytes per second.

362
00:14:10.927 --> 00:14:13.127
And when accessing files that aren't cached in

363
00:14:13.147 --> 00:14:14.948
your file system, the file system needs to first

364
00:14:15.008 --> 00:14:17.228
retrieve the data from the S3 bucket,

365
00:14:17.648 --> 00:14:20.109
which has latencies in the tens of milliseconds.

366
00:14:20.429 --> 00:14:23.429
Data stored in the file system is read with low

367
00:14:23.469 --> 00:14:24.749
sub-millisecond latencies.

368
00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:27.930
Writes are staged on the file system with

369
00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:29.650
single-digit millisecond latencies.

370
00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:33.391
So that's all that AWS has to say about

371
00:14:33.431 --> 00:14:34.051
performance.

372
00:14:34.491 --> 00:14:36.592
My personal experience is that it might be a

373
00:14:36.632 --> 00:14:38.832
little bit tricky to put all these figures

374
00:14:38.872 --> 00:14:39.232
together

375
00:14:39.352 --> 00:14:42.394
depending on the way you use S3 files,

376
00:14:42.454 --> 00:14:45.496
depending on the shape of your data in S3.

377
00:14:45.997 --> 00:14:48.599
Like you have big files, large files.

378
00:14:49.199 --> 00:14:51.240
How much do you access all of them?

379
00:14:51.981 --> 00:14:54.483
How are you setting up the caching layer?

380
00:14:55.003 --> 00:14:57.705
So in reality, these numbers, I would treat them

381
00:14:57.765 --> 00:14:59.706
just as rough guidance, just to have a

382
00:14:59.746 --> 00:15:00.847
high-level understanding.

383
00:15:01.327 --> 00:15:03.849
But I would recommend people to do your own

384
00:15:03.909 --> 00:15:06.571
benchmarks and see, does it really work for your

385
00:15:06.691 --> 00:15:07.192
use cases?

386
00:15:08.012 --> 00:15:11.213
So that I think moves us into our experience.

387
00:15:11.333 --> 00:15:13.514
Eoin, do you want to share anything about that?

388
00:15:14.494 --> 00:15:17.596
Yeah, the nice thing I think there is that

389
00:15:17.876 --> 00:15:20.617
compared to just normal EFS, EFS can be a bit

390
00:15:20.657 --> 00:15:22.617
complex because it's got these different modes.

391
00:15:22.777 --> 00:15:25.378
The latest one is elastic throughput and then

392
00:15:25.398 --> 00:15:27.039
you've got IOPS, right?

393
00:15:27.059 --> 00:15:30.760
You've got provisioned IOPS versus burst,

394
00:15:31.061 --> 00:15:31.921
the old burst method.

395
00:15:33.402 --> 00:15:34.923
can be difficult to get a handle.

396
00:15:35.043 --> 00:15:36.664
And if you do provision IOPS, it can be very

397
00:15:36.704 --> 00:15:37.084
expensive.

398
00:15:39.005 --> 00:15:41.446
So what we wanted to do was just do a

399
00:15:41.466 --> 00:15:42.267
bit of benchmarking.

400
00:15:42.327 --> 00:15:43.928
So we'd have a better idea of when to use

401
00:15:43.968 --> 00:15:45.709
this in our projects as well, when the sweet

402
00:15:45.729 --> 00:15:48.391
spot was, but also to share with everyone.

403
00:15:49.471 --> 00:15:51.532
So we wrote a fairly simple benchmarking

404
00:15:51.552 --> 00:15:52.013
application.

405
00:15:52.833 --> 00:15:55.696
It's not totally scientific because there's

406
00:15:55.716 --> 00:15:58.019
always other influencing factors here.

407
00:15:58.059 --> 00:15:59.400
We tried to make it as simple as possible.

408
00:16:00.161 --> 00:16:02.944
And the approach was we wanted to do reads and

409
00:16:03.444 --> 00:16:05.947
writes of small and large files to S3.

410
00:16:06.287 --> 00:16:09.570
So not that large, but just above the threshold

411
00:16:09.611 --> 00:16:10.451
where they would be cached.

412
00:16:11.172 --> 00:16:12.393
and just measure the performance.

413
00:16:12.873 --> 00:16:14.594
And we also wanted to compare it against all the

414
00:16:14.634 --> 00:16:17.055
different EFS configurations you can have,

415
00:16:17.896 --> 00:16:19.497
and then run it on Lambda and Fargate.

416
00:16:21.058 --> 00:16:22.538
But also, not just Lambda and Fargate,

417
00:16:22.919 --> 00:16:24.680
we wanted to do different CPU and memory

418
00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:25.360
configurations.

419
00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:28.862
So we chose a few different configuration sizes

420
00:16:28.942 --> 00:16:32.904
between 256 megs of memory and 10 gigs of memory

421
00:16:33.284 --> 00:16:34.265
in both Lambda and Fargate.

422
00:16:34.964 --> 00:16:37.166
Now, the repo and results summary will be up on

423
00:16:37.206 --> 00:16:38.948
GitHub if you want to see it for yourself,

424
00:16:38.988 --> 00:16:40.189
and the link will be in the description.

425
00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:45.814
The SAM CloudFormation template we created might

426
00:16:45.834 --> 00:16:48.156
also be useful just if you're figuring out how

427
00:16:48.176 --> 00:16:49.837
do you write one of these things and get it

428
00:16:49.877 --> 00:16:51.119
running yourself with S3 Files.

429
00:16:51.519 --> 00:16:54.101
I think we both had a few cases of trial

430
00:16:54.141 --> 00:16:55.563
and error trying to get the first one working,

431
00:16:55.763 --> 00:16:57.364
so we have something that now works.

432
00:16:58.645 --> 00:16:59.846
So what are our overall findings?

433
00:17:00.126 --> 00:17:02.487
Well, small file writes are generally slightly

434
00:17:02.507 --> 00:17:05.148
faster than S3 put object by about 10 to 20%.

435
00:17:05.508 --> 00:17:06.248
That's what we can see.

436
00:17:07.349 --> 00:17:10.330
The reads are generally dramatically faster than

437
00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:13.451
S3 directly, which you would expect for cached

438
00:17:13.631 --> 00:17:15.832
data, like 5x to 10x faster.

439
00:17:16.573 --> 00:17:19.394
The large file reads are generally much,

440
00:17:19.434 --> 00:17:21.695
much faster with S3 files compared to EFS

441
00:17:21.755 --> 00:17:22.095
options.

442
00:17:23.175 --> 00:17:27.738
And that's because we know it's going through to

443
00:17:28.638 --> 00:17:29.819
streaming directly from S3.

444
00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:32.080
It's still a bit of a surprise to us.

445
00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:33.481
We would have thought that those large file

446
00:17:33.521 --> 00:17:35.882
accesses through the different EFS options,

447
00:17:35.963 --> 00:17:37.343
especially with provisioned IOPS would be

448
00:17:37.363 --> 00:17:39.264
faster, but this is what our results are

449
00:17:39.284 --> 00:17:39.504
showing.

450
00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:43.922
So it's interesting, maybe there's some smart

451
00:17:44.023 --> 00:17:45.464
like parallel fetching going on there in the

452
00:17:45.484 --> 00:17:45.804
background.

453
00:17:46.865 --> 00:17:48.346
In terms of large file writes, there's very

454
00:17:48.386 --> 00:17:48.906
little difference.

455
00:17:51.088 --> 00:17:52.469
It's consistent across the board.

456
00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:55.351
Otherwise, I would say the performance of S3

457
00:17:55.371 --> 00:17:59.454
files is quite similar to EFS with a tiny extra

458
00:17:59.475 --> 00:18:01.396
bit of latency for S3 files.

459
00:18:01.556 --> 00:18:05.960
So unless the only case where S3 files is

460
00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:10.103
significantly faster is in the read operations.

461
00:18:11.611 --> 00:18:15.153
Now, since we tested with various memory and CPU

462
00:18:15.173 --> 00:18:17.115
configurations, it's worth calling out a general

463
00:18:17.175 --> 00:18:19.796
observation, which isn't specific to S3 files.

464
00:18:20.797 --> 00:18:24.079
If you have Fargate or Lambda with 512 megabytes

465
00:18:24.159 --> 00:18:26.881
or less, the network bandwidth really hurts.

466
00:18:27.701 --> 00:18:31.925
And IO can be 10 times worse than just with

467
00:18:31.925 --> 00:18:33.426
2 gigabytes of RAM.

468
00:18:33.947 --> 00:18:36.711
The best performance always came from having 10

469
00:18:36.769 --> 00:18:37.730
gigabytes of RAM.

470
00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:41.373
For Lambda, we know that memory allocation and

471
00:18:41.473 --> 00:18:44.355
network allocation is directly proportion.

472
00:18:44.756 --> 00:18:44.936
Sorry.

473
00:18:45.636 --> 00:18:47.618
For Lambda, we know that memory allocation is

474
00:18:47.658 --> 00:18:49.580
proportionally tied to CPU and network.

475
00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:52.402
And it seems like there's a similar correlation

476
00:18:52.442 --> 00:18:53.022
for Fargate 2.

477
00:18:53.843 --> 00:18:56.184
So if you're doing anything that's IO sensitive,

478
00:18:56.824 --> 00:18:59.725
having a memory allocation of two gigabytes or

479
00:18:59.805 --> 00:19:02.526
more, if possible, will really help you.

480
00:19:03.026 --> 00:19:05.467
The results just start to fall off a cliff if

481
00:19:05.487 --> 00:19:08.008
you try to be really frugal when it comes to

482
00:19:08.208 --> 00:19:09.309
allocating those resources.

483
00:19:10.149 --> 00:19:13.972
There's lots of benefits here, and your mileage

484
00:19:14.012 --> 00:19:15.373
will vary depending on your workload.

485
00:19:15.734 --> 00:19:19.316
But in general, I am saying this is not one

486
00:19:19.336 --> 00:19:20.938
of those services where I'm saying it has a very

487
00:19:21.198 --> 00:19:21.778
narrow niche.

488
00:19:21.818 --> 00:19:23.379
I think it has a broad set of use cases.

489
00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:26.041
If you've got a lot of hot data where

490
00:19:26.081 --> 00:19:27.743
everything's hitting the cache, maybe it's not

491
00:19:27.783 --> 00:19:28.764
going to work very well for you.

492
00:19:29.484 --> 00:19:31.627
But in a normal case, where most of the data

493
00:19:31.647 --> 00:19:33.109
you don't access most of the time,

494
00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:37.214
but you can benefit from some speed up,

495
00:19:37.875 --> 00:19:40.237
especially with small files, and you like the

496
00:19:40.278 --> 00:19:42.801
file system model, then I think it's a real

497
00:19:42.841 --> 00:19:43.081
winner.

498
00:19:44.503 --> 00:19:44.643
Right.

499
00:19:45.023 --> 00:19:46.986
After that, what's the downside?

500
00:19:48.408 --> 00:19:50.551
Yeah, there are quite a few and it's well worth

501
00:19:50.591 --> 00:19:53.414
being aware of them before just jumping straight

502
00:19:53.495 --> 00:19:55.177
into using S3 files for everything.

503
00:19:55.817 --> 00:19:57.740
So the first one we already mentioned to some

504
00:19:57.800 --> 00:20:01.064
extent is that this supports NFS only,

505
00:20:01.485 --> 00:20:04.629
so it doesn't support other network file share

506
00:20:04.789 --> 00:20:06.892
systems or protocols like SMB.

507
00:20:07.973 --> 00:20:09.735
The file system behavior, as we said,

508
00:20:10.095 --> 00:20:12.318
it's trying to do the best that it can possibly

509
00:20:12.358 --> 00:20:15.682
do to fill the gap between what S3 can do

510
00:20:15.882 --> 00:20:18.045
and what a regular file system is supposed to

511
00:20:18.085 --> 00:20:18.205
do.

512
00:20:18.566 --> 00:20:20.468
So there are, of course, things that are missing

513
00:20:20.528 --> 00:20:22.731
or that cannot easily be replicated.

514
00:20:23.811 --> 00:20:26.894
For example, there is no hard links,

515
00:20:27.554 --> 00:20:28.975
there is no atomic renames.

516
00:20:29.916 --> 00:20:34.879
An interesting thing is that if you try,

517
00:20:34.899 --> 00:20:37.361
for instance, to modify a file from the file

518
00:20:37.401 --> 00:20:40.363
system, these changes are staged in the file

519
00:20:40.403 --> 00:20:42.384
system and then eventually they are synchronized

520
00:20:42.404 --> 00:20:42.544
to S3.

521
00:20:43.545 --> 00:20:45.407
which is kind of an interesting optimization,

522
00:20:45.447 --> 00:20:48.289
but comes with some potentially unexpected side

523
00:20:48.329 --> 00:20:50.091
effects that we'll explain in a second.

524
00:20:50.491 --> 00:20:52.113
But the idea is that basically you might be

525
00:20:52.173 --> 00:20:55.556
writing into a few different operations,

526
00:20:56.136 --> 00:20:57.418
files in the local file system.

527
00:20:57.458 --> 00:20:59.960
Maybe the common example is you are appending,

528
00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:03.083
for example, a few times in a few different,

529
00:21:03.363 --> 00:21:04.064
I don't know, seconds.

530
00:21:05.565 --> 00:21:08.166
You wouldn't want, especially if this is a large

531
00:21:08.226 --> 00:21:11.148
file, you wouldn't want that every time you are

532
00:21:11.188 --> 00:21:13.310
doing a change, it immediately tries to

533
00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:14.671
synchronize back to S3.

534
00:21:15.271 --> 00:21:16.952
Because if it's a large file, you are doing a

535
00:21:17.072 --> 00:21:18.993
lot of unnecessary write into S3.

536
00:21:19.734 --> 00:21:23.116
So effectively what S3 files is doing for you,

537
00:21:23.176 --> 00:21:25.197
they are waiting for a certain amount of time to

538
00:21:25.237 --> 00:21:28.339
see, are you doing any other write into this

539
00:21:28.599 --> 00:21:31.301
object before it's actually synchronized back

540
00:21:31.401 --> 00:21:33.382
into one atomic operation into S3.

541
00:21:34.543 --> 00:21:38.766
So the issue here is that you need to be

542
00:21:38.826 --> 00:21:41.208
aware that there is a delay that comes from this

543
00:21:41.248 --> 00:21:41.969
optimization.

544
00:21:42.569 --> 00:21:46.672
So if you are using a mix of access pattern,

545
00:21:46.792 --> 00:21:49.494
for example, if you are reading from the file

546
00:21:49.514 --> 00:21:52.297
system in one place, also writing from the file

547
00:21:52.337 --> 00:21:55.139
system, but then also reading directly from S3

548
00:21:55.199 --> 00:21:57.621
somewhere else, you might see that there is a

549
00:21:57.661 --> 00:21:59.342
little bit of eventual consistency.

550
00:21:59.402 --> 00:22:02.244
So you might not see the new data as soon

551
00:22:02.284 --> 00:22:02.985
as you might expect.

552
00:22:03.445 --> 00:22:04.485
So just be aware of that.

553
00:22:04.565 --> 00:22:06.706
I think the default is 60 seconds.

554
00:22:06.766 --> 00:22:08.246
I'm not even sure if it's something you can

555
00:22:08.386 --> 00:22:08.926
configure.

556
00:22:10.007 --> 00:22:13.807
But yeah, be aware that the consistency isn't

557
00:22:14.488 --> 00:22:14.968
immediate.

558
00:22:15.468 --> 00:22:17.228
So be aware for eventual consistency.

559
00:22:17.268 --> 00:22:17.488
Yeah.

560
00:22:17.768 --> 00:22:20.169
And this 60 second wait, if you've got it,

561
00:22:20.589 --> 00:22:22.349
this is a period where it will wait to see

562
00:22:22.409 --> 00:22:23.810
if there are more write operations.

563
00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:25.770
So if you do have another write operation in

564
00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:28.131
that 60 second period, the 60 seconds will

565
00:22:28.951 --> 00:22:29.391
restart.

566
00:22:29.471 --> 00:22:32.172
So it can take multiple minutes before the file

567
00:22:32.212 --> 00:22:33.232
is actually write in that case.

568
00:22:33.812 --> 00:22:36.093
Yeah, I can imagine that one of the use cases

569
00:22:36.153 --> 00:22:38.415
that I don't know if you are kind of streaming

570
00:22:38.535 --> 00:22:42.517
logs from somewhere and then piping them to the

571
00:22:42.557 --> 00:22:43.517
file system itself.

572
00:22:44.238 --> 00:22:46.599
And these logs are basically streaming for the

573
00:22:46.639 --> 00:22:48.160
entire duration of the application,

574
00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:50.301
you're never going to see that file or those

575
00:22:50.341 --> 00:22:52.262
changes at least being reflected into

576
00:22:53.082 --> 00:22:54.603
the S3 packet itself.

577
00:22:54.703 --> 00:22:56.984
So just be aware that some of these use cases,

578
00:22:57.344 --> 00:22:59.065
you need to really understand the model to see

579
00:22:59.445 --> 00:23:01.526
if everything is going to work for you using the

580
00:23:01.546 --> 00:23:03.947
synchronization primitives that S3 Files

581
00:23:04.047 --> 00:23:04.687
provides for you.

582
00:23:05.527 --> 00:23:08.308
And another thing that I actually bumped myself

583
00:23:08.408 --> 00:23:11.490
in one of the projects we are working with one

584
00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:12.310
of our customers,

585
00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:16.515
is that if you use S3 files and you have

586
00:23:16.535 --> 00:23:20.479
an organization with multiple accounts and maybe

587
00:23:20.499 --> 00:23:23.283
you have a control plan account where you have a

588
00:23:23.363 --> 00:23:25.505
kind of a bucket with data you want to share

589
00:23:25.546 --> 00:23:26.747
with a bunch of other accounts,

590
00:23:28.569 --> 00:23:31.670
You would expect that you could use S3 Files

591
00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:34.192
with a bucket that exists in another account,

592
00:23:34.272 --> 00:23:36.133
even if it's in the same organization and

593
00:23:36.173 --> 00:23:38.514
region, but that doesn't seem to be the case.

594
00:23:39.175 --> 00:23:41.876
I actually couldn't find an explicit mention

595
00:23:41.896 --> 00:23:44.358
that this is a real limitation in the

596
00:23:44.378 --> 00:23:46.739
documentation, but everything I tried just

597
00:23:46.759 --> 00:23:47.339
didn't work.

598
00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:50.121
And so my conclusion is that this feature is not

599
00:23:50.181 --> 00:23:50.921
supported yet.

600
00:23:51.622 --> 00:23:54.403
I'm hoping that it's somewhere in the roadmap in

601
00:23:54.444 --> 00:23:56.385
AWS, because I think it could be very useful.

602
00:23:57.165 --> 00:23:58.806
And just to make you understand why this could

603
00:23:58.826 --> 00:24:00.548
be useful, I want to share a little bit of

604
00:24:00.608 --> 00:24:02.990
the project we are working on and why we thought

605
00:24:03.170 --> 00:24:04.551
this could be a good idea.

606
00:24:05.192 --> 00:24:09.635
So we are building a SaaS application where

607
00:24:10.936 --> 00:24:13.138
different customers, they will get their own

608
00:24:13.438 --> 00:24:14.379
dedicated accounts.

609
00:24:14.459 --> 00:24:18.602
So they can run some kind of modeling workloads.

610
00:24:19.463 --> 00:24:21.045
in their own isolated accounts.

611
00:24:21.485 --> 00:24:24.408
So effectively, the shape of the account is that

612
00:24:24.428 --> 00:24:26.529
there is a central control plane where we have

613
00:24:26.650 --> 00:24:27.690
all the shared resources.

614
00:24:28.651 --> 00:24:31.274
For instance, all the models that the SaaS will

615
00:24:31.354 --> 00:24:33.836
expose are stored into S3, and they are

616
00:24:33.916 --> 00:24:35.557
organized with specific prefixes.

617
00:24:36.178 --> 00:24:38.020
And then every time we onboard a new tenant,

618
00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:39.921
there is a new account that is created,

619
00:24:39.941 --> 00:24:41.383
added to the organization,

620
00:24:42.123 --> 00:24:44.824
and a tenant is going to have access only to

621
00:24:44.864 --> 00:24:46.304
a subset of these models.

622
00:24:46.744 --> 00:24:48.985
so we also need to put in place a mechanism

623
00:24:49.125 --> 00:24:52.406
to basically allow each tenant to read only the

624
00:24:52.466 --> 00:24:55.187
models that they have access to from this kind

625
00:24:55.227 --> 00:24:58.108
of golden bucket that exists in the shared

626
00:24:58.168 --> 00:24:58.868
control plane.

627
00:24:59.388 --> 00:25:01.388
So we thought that S3 Files with the feature

628
00:25:01.408 --> 00:25:03.969
that allows you to mount file systems only on

629
00:25:04.009 --> 00:25:06.170
specific parts would be a good match.

630
00:25:07.010 --> 00:25:09.152
Also because these models are kind of immutable,

631
00:25:09.332 --> 00:25:12.174
so we upload them once, they're never changed.

632
00:25:12.374 --> 00:25:14.255
If they are changed, we upload an entire new

633
00:25:14.295 --> 00:25:16.977
version, so we don't even have all the issues we

634
00:25:17.017 --> 00:25:18.739
just described in terms of synchronizing the

635
00:25:18.759 --> 00:25:19.059
data.

636
00:25:19.579 --> 00:25:21.981
We literally just needed to have an EFS mount

637
00:25:22.681 --> 00:25:25.904
from S3, and S3 Files seems to be pretty good

638
00:25:25.984 --> 00:25:27.345
with all the caching mechanisms.

639
00:25:27.825 --> 00:25:30.367
but we don't suffer from all of the eventual

640
00:25:30.407 --> 00:25:31.027
consistency.

641
00:25:31.688 --> 00:25:35.511
So unfortunately, because the cross account

642
00:25:35.671 --> 00:25:38.573
mount doesn't work, what we needed to do in the

643
00:25:38.673 --> 00:25:41.075
end is to figure out a synchronization mechanism

644
00:25:41.175 --> 00:25:44.578
where we can selectively replicate data from the

645
00:25:44.618 --> 00:25:48.360
central bucket in the control plane to a bucket

646
00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:50.142
that exists in each tenant account,

647
00:25:50.202 --> 00:25:51.363
which is a little bit annoying.

648
00:25:51.823 --> 00:25:52.964
It kind of works in the end.

649
00:25:52.984 --> 00:25:56.126
It just adds a bit of extra complexity that we

650
00:25:56.166 --> 00:25:56.867
didn't want to use.

651
00:25:57.387 --> 00:25:59.689
But otherwise, so far, S3 Files has been working

652
00:25:59.809 --> 00:26:00.250
really well.

653
00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:02.212
We don't really have a benchmark for this

654
00:26:02.232 --> 00:26:04.674
specific project where we're going to have a

655
00:26:04.794 --> 00:26:07.577
quite varied mix of big and small files

656
00:26:07.637 --> 00:26:08.518
depending on the model.

657
00:26:09.119 --> 00:26:12.021
So I think maybe later on, if this is something

658
00:26:12.041 --> 00:26:13.803
interesting, we might do another episode with

659
00:26:13.823 --> 00:26:15.445
the details once we start to use it.

660
00:26:16.025 --> 00:26:18.466
with more and more customers and get some more

661
00:26:18.486 --> 00:26:19.247
realistic data.

662
00:26:19.607 --> 00:26:21.728
But yeah, so far, just now, you cannot do

663
00:26:22.208 --> 00:26:24.349
cross-account mounts, but otherwise,

664
00:26:24.369 --> 00:26:26.370
S3 Files has been working really well for this

665
00:26:26.410 --> 00:26:26.811
project.

666
00:26:27.051 --> 00:26:29.212
So there is one more caveat that I think is

667
00:26:29.252 --> 00:26:32.674
worth mentioning, is that if you rename and move

668
00:26:33.014 --> 00:26:35.895
files a lot, that can affect performance.

669
00:26:36.396 --> 00:26:39.657
And if you have a prefix with millions of

670
00:26:39.757 --> 00:26:42.319
objects, and you've just tried to rename that

671
00:26:42.359 --> 00:26:42.719
prefix,

672
00:26:43.299 --> 00:26:45.180
like just renaming the top folder,

673
00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:48.081
for example, in the file system, that could take

674
00:26:48.101 --> 00:26:48.501
hours.

675
00:26:49.301 --> 00:26:52.022
So there's just something to be aware.

676
00:26:52.082 --> 00:26:53.642
And I think there is also some kind of

677
00:26:53.762 --> 00:26:57.623
limitation, right, on like AWS will warn you

678
00:26:58.204 --> 00:27:00.484
that that might happen and you need to accept

679
00:27:00.604 --> 00:27:01.705
the warning explicitly.

680
00:27:01.945 --> 00:27:02.125
Yeah.

681
00:27:02.905 --> 00:27:05.708
If you've, it will actually look at how many

682
00:27:05.768 --> 00:27:07.970
objects are in your bucket before you create a

683
00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:08.411
file system.

684
00:27:08.531 --> 00:27:11.033
And if you've got like, the documentation says

685
00:27:11.093 --> 00:27:12.915
if you've got something like 12 million objects,

686
00:27:13.496 --> 00:27:13.676
that

687
00:27:13.816 --> 00:27:16.198
means there's a potential for a four hour rename

688
00:27:16.238 --> 00:27:16.699
operation.

689
00:27:17.379 --> 00:27:19.822
And it'll give you an error unless you accept a

690
00:27:20.463 --> 00:27:22.785
warning saying, you know, it's okay,

691
00:27:22.845 --> 00:27:25.608
I'm willing to accept the risk and avoid it.

692
00:27:26.528 --> 00:27:27.929
So, this is technically something that might

693
00:27:28.029 --> 00:27:31.231
fail at deployment if you accidentally...

694
00:27:31.331 --> 00:27:34.373
Unless you add an explicit accept warning

695
00:27:34.433 --> 00:27:34.854
property

696
00:27:34.954 --> 00:27:34.994
to

697
00:27:35.014 --> 00:27:35.834
your configuration.

698
00:27:36.935 --> 00:27:37.215
Okay.

699
00:27:37.235 --> 00:27:39.817
So, hopefully that gives you an idea of some of

700
00:27:39.917 --> 00:27:42.579
the trade-offs and the limitations and missing

701
00:27:42.599 --> 00:27:43.059
features.

702
00:27:43.219 --> 00:27:45.360
So, you are a little bit more informed when you

703
00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:47.822
decide to use S3 files for your projects,

704
00:27:48.142 --> 00:27:50.424
but I think we need to talk about cost.

705
00:27:50.784 --> 00:27:52.305
So, what's the story there?

706
00:27:52.913 --> 00:27:54.894
OK, well, you've got the S3 pricing.

707
00:27:54.914 --> 00:27:56.454
You're always going to pay for that under the

708
00:27:56.474 --> 00:27:56.654
hood.

709
00:27:57.394 --> 00:28:00.255
Then on top of that, data stored in your cache

710
00:28:00.496 --> 00:28:03.537
is priced at around 30 cents per gigabyte or

711
00:28:03.597 --> 00:28:04.637
more, depending on the region.

712
00:28:05.217 --> 00:28:06.638
What's the story with Sao Paulo?

713
00:28:06.798 --> 00:28:10.319
It's like 57 cents compared to 30 cents in US

714
00:28:10.359 --> 00:28:10.499
East 1.

715
00:28:11.813 --> 00:28:12.153
I don't know.

716
00:28:13.234 --> 00:28:16.035
Brazilian listeners, please tell us how you feel

717
00:28:16.075 --> 00:28:18.176
about this and what you do about it.

718
00:28:18.797 --> 00:28:21.598
Reads from the cache are around $0.03 per

719
00:28:21.798 --> 00:28:22.158
gigabyte.

720
00:28:22.178 --> 00:28:24.560
Again, that starts at that price.

721
00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:26.421
That's reads from a cache, right?

722
00:28:26.441 --> 00:28:27.261
$0.03 per gigabyte.

723
00:28:27.961 --> 00:28:30.683
And file writes are around $0.06 or more per

724
00:28:30.703 --> 00:28:31.743
gigabyte, 6 cents or 7 cents per gigabyte.

725
00:28:35.405 --> 00:28:39.046
Now, if you're familiar with EFS elastic

726
00:28:39.066 --> 00:28:41.986
throughput pricing, you might notice that those

727
00:28:42.026 --> 00:28:43.447
prices are the exact same.

728
00:28:44.107 --> 00:28:46.147
The main difference is that files that aren't in

729
00:28:46.187 --> 00:28:48.848
your cache are read from S3 with no additional

730
00:28:48.928 --> 00:28:49.188
cost.

731
00:28:50.228 --> 00:28:52.288
So that's the way to think about it.

732
00:28:52.848 --> 00:28:55.669
There is an additional cost here, but compared

733
00:28:55.689 --> 00:29:00.090
to EFS, there's a huge saving for reads that

734
00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:00.950
don't hit your cache.

735
00:29:01.630 --> 00:29:03.532
So in practice this means you can save a lot

736
00:29:03.572 --> 00:29:06.194
of money compared to EFS but there is a premium

737
00:29:06.234 --> 00:29:07.356
compared to just using S3.

738
00:29:07.656 --> 00:29:09.538
In exchange for that you get fast read and write

739
00:29:09.578 --> 00:29:12.420
performance especially on cached small files.

740
00:29:13.041 --> 00:29:15.343
If all or most of your data is small and

741
00:29:15.403 --> 00:29:17.225
frequently accessed it can end up all in the

742
00:29:17.245 --> 00:29:19.527
cache so the costs can mount up and maybe it

743
00:29:19.567 --> 00:29:20.408
doesn't make sense there.

744
00:29:21.168 --> 00:29:23.589
On the other hand, if most data is larger or

745
00:29:23.689 --> 00:29:26.309
less frequently accessed, you might have the

746
00:29:26.329 --> 00:29:27.729
sweet spot for S3 files.

747
00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:29.650
One thing we didn't mention yet is that this

748
00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:31.830
will also work with all the different access

749
00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:34.211
tiers in S3, like infrequently accessed,

750
00:29:34.291 --> 00:29:35.231
and Glacier, and everything.

751
00:29:35.971 --> 00:29:39.912
So you can still get those cost savings in your

752
00:29:39.972 --> 00:29:40.772
S3 layer.

753
00:29:42.012 --> 00:29:44.593
And yeah, otherwise, there are those trade-offs.

754
00:29:44.653 --> 00:29:46.833
You talked about Luciano, particularly the big

755
00:29:46.893 --> 00:29:49.374
one, which is this 60-second write-back delay.

756
00:29:50.619 --> 00:29:53.180
OK, so let's try to wrap this episode up.

757
00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:55.120
I'm going to try to do a quick recap first

758
00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:57.401
and then give you our final take.

759
00:29:58.301 --> 00:30:00.022
So S3 is amazing.

760
00:30:00.502 --> 00:30:03.223
We all use it in all kinds of use cases.

761
00:30:03.903 --> 00:30:06.063
But the reality is that it's not a file system.

762
00:30:06.083 --> 00:30:08.384
So there are still use cases where applications

763
00:30:08.424 --> 00:30:10.065
are expected to have a file system.

764
00:30:10.545 --> 00:30:12.405
So you need to bridge the gap somehow.

765
00:30:12.685 --> 00:30:15.386
And S3 Files is a new way to do that.

766
00:30:15.926 --> 00:30:17.267
And it is pretty promising.

767
00:30:19.227 --> 00:30:21.709
it basically lets you mount S3-backed storage

768
00:30:22.450 --> 00:30:26.212
into EC2, ECS, EKS, and even Lambda using EFS

769
00:30:26.252 --> 00:30:28.474
concepts that you might have seen already if you

770
00:30:28.614 --> 00:30:30.415
used EFS as a service.

771
00:30:31.176 --> 00:30:33.658
And this is not strange when you realize that

772
00:30:33.758 --> 00:30:37.340
under the hood, AWS is using EFS as a caching

773
00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:40.963
layer between reading from S3 directly and

774
00:30:41.023 --> 00:30:43.865
making that data available as a file system into

775
00:30:43.885 --> 00:30:46.067
EC2, ECS, EKS, or whatever.

776
00:30:46.767 --> 00:30:50.149
So that's why you can see a lot of EFS

777
00:30:50.409 --> 00:30:50.769
things.

778
00:30:51.249 --> 00:30:53.130
And it might be very familiar if you have used

779
00:30:53.510 --> 00:30:53.930
EFS.

780
00:30:54.290 --> 00:30:57.072
I think that the interesting story there is that

781
00:30:57.412 --> 00:30:59.533
you can keep S3 as the source of truth,

782
00:31:00.033 --> 00:31:01.894
but give the application a more traditional

783
00:31:01.954 --> 00:31:03.575
system interface when they need it.

784
00:31:03.995 --> 00:31:06.756
And you can also leverage this caching layer for

785
00:31:06.856 --> 00:31:09.157
improving performance whenever you,

786
00:31:09.277 --> 00:31:11.038
for instance, are reading lots of small files.

787
00:31:11.958 --> 00:31:15.020
And also if you were using EFS before,

788
00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:17.661
there is a potential here to save money because

789
00:31:17.701 --> 00:31:19.562
you are not always reading from EFS,

790
00:31:19.602 --> 00:31:21.863
you are not always keeping all the data into

791
00:31:21.923 --> 00:31:24.284
EFS, which is generally where most of the cost

792
00:31:24.324 --> 00:31:27.506
would come from if you use EFS with lots of

793
00:31:27.546 --> 00:31:28.667
big files, for example.

794
00:31:29.647 --> 00:31:32.951
So our benchmark suggests that S3 files can be

795
00:31:33.511 --> 00:31:36.034
quite interesting for workloads where you're

796
00:31:36.054 --> 00:31:39.077
doing lots of small file reads, and effectively,

797
00:31:39.337 --> 00:31:42.060
in those cases, if you would read directly from

798
00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:43.962
S3, that might impact performance.

